# Instructions

For the target at checks.{{ name }}, is its explicitly attributed speaker a DIFFERENT participant from the speaker of the genuine immediately preceding spoken turn?

Use the full three-page local_raw_evidence and preceding_source_index to identify the genuine preceding MAIN-TEXT utterance. Ownership and immediate_paragraph_hint are fallible locators, not a fixed edge. Exclude ancillary material and do not invent participants or text beyond the supplied pages.

Ordinary quotation marks can enclose terms, titles or cited passages rather than dialogue. Do not certify a spoken turn from quotation marks, a new sentence, a conjunction or topical coherence alone.

Resolve each speaker from actual reporting words and nearby dialogue context. A question addressed to another participant and that participant's expressly attributed reply can establish different speakers. A continued utterance by the same person, a new name merely mentioned in dialogue, or a quoted passage from an external source does not establish a speaker change.

Do not infer a different speaker solely from different pronouns, quotation marks, an odd opening or a change of subject. If either identity or the actual turn sequence cannot be established from the supplied neighborhood, answer FALSE. This is a participant-identity question only, not a typesetting judgment.

# True

The actual preceding and target spoken turns are attributed to distinct participants.

# False

A change between identifiable speakers is not established; this may be the same speaker, a non-dialogue quotation or missing context.
